      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "$\\,$ $x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}$\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}) & ≡¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3}∧y>\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3})∨¬(y>\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ≡x>\\frac{1}{3}∨y\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ≡x>\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ or }y\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
